#!/bin/sh
# script/fastpath-footprint: the IPC fastpath must stay small enough to live in L1i.
#
# **Why this exists, and it is the oldest performance argument in microkernels.** Liedtke's
# "On micro-Kernel Construction" (SOSP 1995) argued that Mach's IPC was slow because of the CACHE
# FOOTPRINT of its hot path rather than anything inherent to microkernels: a kernel that touches a
# lot of memory per IPC evicts the *application's* working set, so the cost shows up as capacity
# misses spread through the workload instead of as time in the kernel. L4 was written against that,
# and seL4 keeps a hand-maintained fastpath for the same reason. notes/benchmarks.md has the full
# argument, the frontier cache sizes it is measured against, and the target;
# design/roadmap/132-the-fastpath-footprint.md is the milestone that owns this gate.
#
# **What this measures**: an UPPER BOUND on the instruction footprint of an IPC round trip, in two
# parts, because they are two different quantities and averaging them would hide both.
#
#   ipc_fastpath   the transitive closure of non-cold calls from the IPC and switch roots. Whole
#                  symbol sizes, so a cold tail inside a hot function counts. Upper bound.
#   syscall_entry  the trap vector, the exception dispatcher and `syscall::dispatch`, FLAT with no
#                  closure. A syscall traverses one path through the decoder, but the decoder's own
#                  bytes are on every syscall, and its other arms are not on this path at all.
#
# **`syscall_entry` is not like for like across the three ISAs, and the reason is architectural.**
# aarch64 and riscv64 route a syscall through the same vector table and cause decoder as every other
# exception, so their entry numbers carry the whole trap path. x86_64 does not: `syscall` jumps
# through `IA32_LSTAR` and never touches the 256-entry IDT, so its entry number covers four symbols
# and legitimately excludes 3,330 bytes of stubs and trap handlers that the other two ISAs' figures
# have no equivalent of. x86_64's entry figure being the smallest of the three is that fact, not a
# leaner decoder. The per-symbol reasoning is at `ENTRY` below. `ipc_fastpath` IS comparable across
# all three: it closes over portable Rust and the symbol sets agree.
#
# **What it is not.** It is not a cache measurement. No cache is modelled by icount and the HVF host
# has an L1i several times larger than the boards, so nothing in this tree can yet observe the
# effect this bounds. Milestone 74's PMU counters on milestone 127's Jetson TX1 are what turn this
# from arithmetic into measurement. Until then this is a tripwire on the quantity, not the harm.
#
# BUGS
#   **This measured two of the three architectures until 2026-08-27**, with nothing in the script
#   recording the omission, which is the same gap `script/stack-frame-check` had and the
#   architecture-list sweep found. x86_64 is measured now. Anything added here that is per-architecture
#   (`ENTRY`, the `case` below, `CALL`) has to be extended when a fourth ISA arrives, and the
#   `ENTRY` set is a judgement about that ISA's trap architecture rather than a string edit.
#
# Usage:
#   script/fastpath-footprint            # measure all three ISAs and check against the baselines
#   script/fastpath-footprint --save     # re-record the baselines (a statement that a change is intended)
#   script/fastpath-footprint --arch aarch64
#   script/fastpath-footprint --features fastpath_pad  # E3 (milestone 134): build with an extra
#                                         # kernel feature (comma-separated for more than one) and
#                                         # measure THAT binary. Never implies --save: a padded
#                                         # build's numbers are an experiment's reading, not a new
#                                         # baseline, so they print and are never checked or written.
#
# Name: unrecorded, and provisional like everything a lane mints. `fastpath` is the field's own word
# for the thing being measured: it is what seL4 calls its hand-written common-case IPC path, so a
# reader who knows the literature meets a term they already have. `footprint` is the quantity rather
# than the verdict, which is deliberate: `-check` is this directory's suffix for a gate that fails a
# build (`interleaving-check`, `qemu-check`, `stack-frame-check`), and by that convention this should
# probably be `fastpath-footprint-check`. It is not, because the name would then be nine syllables to
# say a thing calef may want spelled differently anyway. Worth settling; nothing depends on it.
#
# See notes/benchmarks.md and script/stack-frame-check, whose shape this copies: derive a number
# from the built kernel, compare it against a rule, fail the build. That gate exists because
# `sched::reap_region_objects` grew a 6816-byte frame that compiled without a warning and was found
# only when CI faulted one run in five. A hot-path footprint has the same shape: it grows silently.
#
# BUGS
#
#   - **It gates two of the three supported architectures.** `arches` below is `aarch64 riscv64`
#     and the `case` exits 2 on anything else, so x86_64's IPC fastpath is unmeasured. That is an
#     omission rather than a decision: x86_64 became a `script/test` target on 2026-08-24
#     (milestone 161 item 4) and nobody widened this list. The sibling gate this file copies its
#     shape from settled the same gap on 2026-08-27 (see `script/stack-frame-check`'s own BUGS,
#     which makes x86_64 reachable via `--arch` while holding it out of the default set until a
#     found offender is dispositioned), and that is the shape to follow here.
#   - **Closing it needs two files that do not exist**, so it is not a one-word widening:
#     `bench/fastpath-x86_64.txt` (the baseline, where both other architectures have one) and
#     `kernel/src/arch/x86_64/fastpath_pad.rs` (which `--features fastpath_pad` needs). The two
#     tripwire file sets diverged along the way: `bench/baseline-<arch>.txt` is complete at three
#     and `bench/fastpath-<arch>.txt` is at two. The `ROOTS` table below also needs an x86_64 row,
#     naming that ISA's trap-entry and dispatch symbols.
#   - See notes/architecture-list-sweep.md, finding 11, and milestone 186, which owns this.

set -e
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."

save=""
arches="aarch64 riscv64 x86_64"
features=""
while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    case "$1" in
        --save) save=1 ;;
        --arch) shift; arches="$1" ;;
        --features) shift; features="$1" ;;
        *) echo "script/fastpath-footprint: unknown argument $1" >&2; exit 2 ;;
    esac
    shift
done

# **`--features` never gates and never saves.** A padded build's footprint is an experiment's
# reading (E3, design/roadmap/134-the-measurements-that-decide.md), not a candidate baseline: it is
# expected to fail a 5% tolerance built for the un-padded kernel, and checking it would just print
# the failure the experiment is deliberately causing. `--save` with `--features` would silently
# replace the real baseline with an experimental one, which is the mistake this refuses outright.
if [ -n "$features" ] && [ -n "$save" ]; then
    echo "script/fastpath-footprint: --save with --features would overwrite the baseline with an experimental build; not doing that" >&2
    exit 2
fi

tools="$(rustc --print sysroot)/lib/rustlib/$(rustc -vV | grep '^host:' | cut -d' ' -f2)/bin"
if [ ! -x "$tools/llvm-objdump" ]; then
    echo "script/fastpath-footprint: llvm-objdump not found; run script/bootstrap (llvm-tools)." >&2
    exit 1
fi

status=0
# shellcheck disable=SC2086  # $arches is a deliberate space-separated list; POSIX sh has no arrays
for arch in $arches; do
    case "$arch" in
        aarch64) target=aarch64-unknown-none-softfloat ;;
        riscv64) target=riscv64imac-unknown-none-elf ;;
        # No RUSTFLAGS is set anywhere in this script, which is what makes this arm a one-liner:
        # `x86_64-unknown-none` needs `-C relocation-model=static` (a kernel cannot be a static-PIE)
        # and gets it from `.cargo/config.toml`. A bare `RUSTFLAGS` in the environment REPLACES that
        # table rather than appending to it, and rust-lld then fails with `relocation R_X86_64_32
        # cannot be used against local symbol`. Found by the `script/stack-frame-check` lane that
        # widened it the same week; if this script ever grows a RUSTFLAGS of its own, re-add the
        # flag explicitly.
        x86_64) target=x86_64-unknown-none ;;
        *) echo "script/fastpath-footprint: no target for arch $arch" >&2; exit 2 ;;
    esac

    # **The RELEASE binary, and that is the whole point.** A debug kernel's `.text` is about twice
    # the size (notes/benchmarks.md measured 348 KiB against 168 KiB on aarch64), so a debug
    # footprint would be measuring the absence of the optimizer rather than the shape of the path.
    if [ -n "$features" ]; then
        echo "==> $arch: building the release kernel (--features $features)"
        cargo build -p kernel --release --target "$target" --features "$features" >/dev/null
    else
        echo "==> $arch: building the release kernel"
        cargo build -p kernel --release --target "$target" >/dev/null
    fi

    tmp=$(mktemp -d)
    trap 'rm -rf "$tmp"' EXIT
    "$tools/llvm-objdump" -d --no-show-raw-insn "target/$target/release/kernel" > "$tmp/dis.txt"

    ARCH="$arch" DIS="$tmp/dis.txt" BASE="bench/fastpath-$arch.txt" SAVE="$save" \
        NOCHECK="$features" python3 - <<'PYEOF' || status=1
import os, re, sys, collections

arch, dis_path, base_path, save = os.environ["ARCH"], os.environ["DIS"], os.environ["BASE"], os.environ["SAVE"]

# **Local labels are not symbols.** The riscv build emits 1,994 `.Lpcrel_hi*` headers, which would
# otherwise chop real functions into fragments and make every size wrong. Skipping them keeps the
# enclosing function accumulating, which is what a footprint wants.
SYMHDR = re.compile(r"^([0-9a-f]+) <(.+)>:$")
# aarch64 calls with `bl`; riscv64 with `jalr` (and `jal`); x86_64 with `callq`. All three
# tail-call with their unconditional branch (`b` / `j` / `jmp`). Every form objdump annotates with a
# target symbol is followed, because a tail call is still a fetch of the callee's bytes.
#
# **Conditional branches are deliberately not here, on any of the three.** `b.ne`, `bne` and `jne`
# all fail the `\s` after the mnemonic, which was an accident of the original two-ISA pattern and is
# now a checked choice: every conditional branch in the x86_64 kernel's five root symbols targets an
# offset inside the branching symbol itself (`<ipc_send+0x2b8>`), which the `t != cur` test below
# would discard anyway. Counting them would add no edge and would risk following a cold arm.
CALL = re.compile(r"\b(bl|blr|jal|jalr|b|j|callq?|jmpq?)\s+[^<]*<([^>+]+)")

start, last, calls = {}, {}, collections.defaultdict(set)
cur = None
for line in open(dis_path, encoding="utf8", errors="replace"):
    line = line.rstrip("\n")
    m = SYMHDR.match(line)
    if m:
        name = m.group(2)
        if name.startswith(".L"):
            continue          # a local label inside the current function, not a new one
        cur = name
        start.setdefault(cur, int(m.group(1), 16))
        continue
    if cur is None:
        continue
    a = re.match(r"^([0-9a-f]+):", line)
    if a:
        last[cur] = int(a.group(1), 16)
    c = CALL.search(line)
    if c:
        t = c.group(2)
        if not t.startswith(".L") and t != cur:
            calls[cur].add(t)

# The last instruction's address plus its width. 4 on aarch64; riscv64 mixes 2- and 4-byte
# instructions, so 4 is the conservative choice, which is the right direction for an upper bound.
#
# **x86_64 is the case where 4 is a guess in both directions**, since its instructions are 1 to 15
# bytes, and it was worth measuring rather than asserting. Summing the exact sizes instead (the gap
# to the next symbol header, padding included) gives 6,619 bytes against this formula's 6,639 for the
# closure and 1,632 against 1,637 for the entry set: a 0.3% over-count. Two effects nearly cancel.
# LLVM parks `int3` padding after most x86 functions and objdump lists those bytes as instructions,
# so `last` is usually already the final padding byte and the +4 lands past the symbol; against that,
# a function ending in a 5-byte tail `jmp` is under-counted by one. 0.3% is a fifteenth of the
# tolerance below, so this is noise rather than a bound that needs a different formula.
size = {s: last.get(s, start[s]) - start[s] + 4 for s in start}

# **The roots: what "the IPC fastpath" is.** This list is a decision, not a heuristic, and it is the
# question notes/benchmarks.md named as the thing blocking this gate from existing. Matched by
# regex because mangled names carry a per-target crate disambiguator that is not stable.
ROOTS = re.compile(r"kernel5sched(8ipc_send|8ipc_recv|8schedule|13finish_switch|11current_cap)")

# **Cold, so excluded from the closure.** Two families, for two different reasons.
#
# Panic and formatting: reached only when the kernel is already dying, so those bytes are never
# fetched on a healthy IPC. LLVM parks them at the end of a function, on their own cache lines.
#
# Teardown: `finish_switch` reaps a *finished* thread, which drags in `KernelStack::drop`,
# `untyped::destroy`, `revoke_region`, `delete_frame_caps` and the unmap path. That branch runs when
# a thread exits and never during an IPC. Including it put 11.2 KiB on this number when the honest
# figure is 5.6, which is exactly the over-count that would make the gate meaningless.
COLD = re.compile(
    r"panic|expect_failed|unwrap_failed|slice_index|9panicking|4core3fmt|assert"
    r"|6revoke|7destroy|drop_in_place|delete_frame_caps|unmap|KernelStack|12AddressSpace"
)

# **The entry set, and x86_64 is not the same shape as the other two.** On aarch64 and riscv64 a
# syscall IS an exception: `svc` lands in the same vector table as a page fault, `ecall` in the same
# `stvec` handler as a timer interrupt, so the whole vector and the whole cause decoder are bytes
# every syscall fetches and both lists carry them. On x86_64 a ring-3 `syscall` reads `IA32_LSTAR`
# and jumps, consulting **no IDT entry at all** (kernel/src/arch/x86_64/trap.s says so at its
# `x86_syscall_entry` header: "THIS IS NOT AN IDT VECTOR AND THE DIFFERENCES ALL MATTER"). So the
# correct x86_64 set is NARROWER than the other two, not wider, and a reader comparing the three
# numbers should not read the difference as this port having a leaner decoder.
#
# Included, each because a `syscall` fetches its bytes:
#   x86_syscall_entry    the `IA32_LSTAR` target, written by `init_syscall` in exceptions.rs. The
#                        hardware jumps straight here.
#   x86_syscall_handler  "the one Rust function every `syscall` reaches" (exceptions.rs), which
#                        bumps the counter and tail-calls the portable dispatcher.
#   isr_restore          the return path. `x86_syscall_entry` ends `jmp isr_restore`, and trap.s
#                        names it "the RISC-V twin of `trap_return`", which riscv64's list already
#                        carries for the same reason. Shared with the IDT path; a syscall runs it.
#   syscall::dispatch    as on both other architectures.
#
# Excluded, each because a `syscall` never reaches it:
#   isr_0 .. isr_255     the 256 IDT stubs, 2,412 bytes of them. Reached only through an IDT gate.
#   isr_common           the stubs' shared save path, which `x86_syscall_entry` duplicates inline
#                        rather than jumping to (it has a different frame to reconstruct).
#   x86_trap_dispatch,   the Rust trap handlers. These ARE the twins of riscv64's
#   x86_trap_body        `riscv_trap_dispatch` / `riscv_trap_body`, which riscv64's list includes,
#                        and that is exactly the asymmetry: on riscv64 the syscall arrives through
#                        them, on x86_64 it does not. Including them here for symmetry with the
#                        other list would put 918 bytes on this number that no syscall fetches.
#   user_return,         first entry to ring 3, and the interrupt-stack trampoline. Once per thread
#   dispatch_on_...      and per IDT trap respectively, neither per syscall.
ENTRY = {
    "aarch64": [r"^exception_vectors$", r"^exception_dispatch$", r"kernel7syscall8dispatch"],
    "riscv64": [r"^trap_entry$", r"^trap_return$", r"^riscv_trap_dispatch$", r"^riscv_trap_body$",
                r"kernel7syscall8dispatch"],
    "x86_64": [r"^x86_syscall_entry$", r"^x86_syscall_handler$", r"^isr_restore$",
               r"kernel7syscall8dispatch"],
}[arch]

roots = [s for s in size if ROOTS.search(s)]
if not roots:
    print(f"fastpath-footprint: {arch}: no root symbols matched; the kernel or the names moved", file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(1)

seen, stack = set(), list(roots)
while stack:
    s = stack.pop()
    if s in seen or s not in size or COLD.search(s):
        continue
    seen.add(s)
    stack.extend(calls.get(s, ()))
closure_total = sum(size[s] for s in seen)

entry_syms = [s for s in size if any(re.search(p, s) for p in ENTRY)]
entry_total = sum(size[s] for s in entry_syms)
total = closure_total + entry_total

print(f"    ipc_fastpath  {closure_total:6d} bytes over {len(seen)} symbols")
print(f"    syscall_entry {entry_total:6d} bytes over {len(entry_syms)} symbols (flat, no closure)")
print(f"    total         {total:6d} bytes ({total/1024:.2f} KiB), an upper bound")

nocheck = os.environ.get("NOCHECK", "")
if nocheck:
    # E3 (milestone 134): a padded build reports and never gates, and it reads against the real
    # baseline for a human rather than pretending it has none. The comparison is informational
    # only; `sys.exit(0)` regardless of the ratio, because failing here would just be
    # `fastpath-footprint`'s own 5% tripwire firing on a build that was deliberately made to trip
    # it, which tells nobody anything they did not already know from asking for `--features`.
    if os.path.exists(base_path):
        want = {}
        for line in open(base_path):
            line = line.strip()
            if line and not line.startswith("#"):
                k, v = line.split()
                want[k] = int(v)
        for key, got in (("ipc_fastpath", closure_total), ("syscall_entry", entry_total)):
            ref = want.get(key)
            if ref:
                print(f"    {key}: {got/ref:.2f}x the un-padded baseline ({ref} -> {got})")
    else:
        print(f"    (no baseline at {base_path} to compare against)")
    sys.exit(0)

if save:
    with open(base_path, "w") as f:
        f.write(
            "# bench/fastpath-%s.txt: an upper bound on the IPC fastpath's instruction footprint.\n"
            "# Written by script/fastpath-footprint --save. Updating this file is a statement that a\n"
            "# footprint change is intended and understood; do it in the commit that causes it.\n"
            "# See notes/benchmarks.md for what the two numbers mean and what they are measured against.\n"
            "ipc_fastpath %d\nsyscall_entry %d\n" % (arch, closure_total, entry_total)
        )
    print(f"    wrote {base_path}")
    sys.exit(0)

if not os.path.exists(base_path):
    print(f"fastpath-footprint: {arch}: no baseline at {base_path}; run --save", file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(1)

want = {}
for line in open(base_path):
    line = line.strip()
    if line and not line.startswith("#"):
        k, v = line.split()
        want[k] = int(v)

# **Five percent, tighter than the icount tripwire's ten**, and the reason is that these numbers are
# static. icount drifts across builds because the compiler remakes inlining decisions for unrelated
# reasons (notes/benchmarks.md records that measurement); a symbol size moves when the code moves.
# A looser bound here would let real creep through.
#
# **Symmetric on purpose** (milestone 156). A one-sided `delta > TOL` only ever catches growth, so
# a real improvement (an extraction, a smaller data structure) can land and sit unclaimed as slack
# in the baseline forever, and a stale-loose baseline silently widens the tripwire's real tolerance:
# if the code shrinks 20% and nobody re-saves, a later regression of +15% against the *current* code
# is actually +15% against a number 20% too generous, and the gate still says "within bound." A
# shrink needs no justification for *why* (unlike growth, which must say what caused it), only an
# explicit `--save` that locks in the tighter number, so the baseline never drifts stale by more
# than one tolerance band in either direction. Measured run-to-run variance on an un-padded build is
# 0% (three back-to-back runs with no code change produced byte-identical symbol sizes, since a
# release build has no incremental-compilation or codegen-unit nondeterminism to speak of), so TOL
# is not narrower than anything the data supports in either direction.
TOL = 0.05
bad = False
for key, got in (("ipc_fastpath", closure_total), ("syscall_entry", entry_total)):
    ref = want.get(key)
    if ref is None:
        print(f"fastpath-footprint: {arch}: baseline has no {key}; run --save", file=sys.stderr)
        bad = True
        continue
    delta = (got - ref) / ref if ref else 0.0
    if delta > TOL:
        print(f"fastpath-footprint: {arch}: {key} grew {delta*100:.1f}% ({ref} -> {got}), over the {TOL*100:.0f}% bound.",
              file=sys.stderr)
        print("  Shrink it, or re-record with --save in the commit that causes the growth and say why.",
              file=sys.stderr)
        bad = True
    elif delta < -TOL:
        print(f"fastpath-footprint: {arch}: {key} shrank {-delta*100:.1f}% ({ref} -> {got}), over the {TOL*100:.0f}% bound.",
              file=sys.stderr)
        print("  The baseline is stale: re-record with --save to lock in the tighter bound (no justification needed for shrinking, just an explicit acknowledgment).",
              file=sys.stderr)
        bad = True
    elif abs(delta) > 0.001:
        print(f"    {key}: {delta*100:+.1f}% against baseline ({ref}), within bound")
sys.exit(1 if bad else 0)
PYEOF
    rm -rf "$tmp"
    trap - EXIT
done

[ "$status" = 0 ] && [ -z "$save" ] && [ -z "$features" ] && echo "fastpath-footprint: within bound on every ISA checked"
[ "$status" = 0 ] && [ -n "$features" ] && echo "fastpath-footprint: measured (--features $features; not checked against the baseline)"
exit "$status"
